---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/paths';
---
<Layout title="Developer guide · Playground.exe" active="guide"><article class="guide max-w-[760px] p-[65px_0_75px] [&_h1]:mt-[20px] [&_section]:[border-top:1px_solid_var(--line)] [&_section]:pt-[27px] [&_section]:mt-[34px] [&_h2]:text-[22px] [&_h2]:mb-[16px] [&_p]:text-[14px] [&_p]:text-muted [&_p]:m-[15px_0] [&_code]:text-[#d4bff6] [&_code]:text-[12px] max-[760px]:pt-[45px] max-[500px]:[&_h1]:text-[42px]"><p class="eyebrow font-sans text-[10px] font-semibold tracking-[1.8px] text-muted">FROM IDEA TO IFRAME</p><h1>Your next game<br/><span>starts here.</span></h1><p class="intro-copy text-[14px] text-muted leading-[1.8] m-[22px_0] max-[760px]:text-[12px] max-[500px]:text-[13px]">A small toolkit. Your choice of engine. A place to publish.</p><section><h2>01 / Make a space</h2><p>From the project root, create a game and install its workspace dependencies.</p><pre><code>npm run new:game my-game
npm install
npm run dev:game my-game</code></pre><p>The template contains a blank canvas and lifecycle hooks. No game mechanics are included. Your game lives in <code>games/my-game/</code> and has its own Vite development server.</p></section><section><h2>02 / Make it yours</h2><p>Edit <code>game.json</code> with a title, description, genre, author, version, publish date, tags, and controls. The ID must match the folder name. An optional <code>cover</code> points to an image in the game's <code>public/</code> directory, such as <code>cover.webp</code>.</p><p>Import utilities from <code>@playground-exe/game-kit</code>: lifecycle hooks, pointer and keyboard input, canvas scaling, audio, asset loading, namespaced storage, and a website message bridge. Use Canvas, DOM, or your preferred engine.</p><pre><code>const game = createGame('my-game', lifecycle);
await game.boot();
game.score(100);
game.requestPause();</code></pre></section><section><h2>03 / Build and publish</h2><pre><code>npm run check
npm test
npm run build
npm run preview</code></pre><p>The build discovers game metadata, bundles each game, and creates static game pages. Restart the website development server after adding a game; rebuild games to update embedded players.</p><p>Push to <code>main</code> and select <strong>GitHub Actions</strong> in your repository's Pages settings. The deployment workflow configures your repository's base path automatically. See the repository README for custom domains and optional Phaser setup.</p></section><section><h2>04 / Bring your friends</h2><p>Start with a multiplayer-ready template, or add multiplayer modes and a server rules module to an existing game.</p><pre><code>npm run new:game my-game -- --multiplayer
npm install
npm run build:cloudflare
npm run dev:party</code></pre><p>Open <a href={withBase('party/')}>the Party page</a> to create a party or join by code. The host chooses a game and mode; players take seats and ready up. Player limits belong to each mode. Everyone else can watch or sit out.</p><pre><code>{`const session = game.multiplayer();
session.onState(state => render(state));
session.onPlayers(players => renderPlayers(players));
session.send('place-piece', { x: 2, y: 3 });`}</code></pre><p>The shared kit handles connections and reconnects. Your <code>src/multiplayer.ts</code> module defines validated server actions and per-player views. Use <code>pocket-circuit</code> as the reference for turns, shared challenges, live movement, and transitions from solo practice to shared play. Full API and deployment instructions are in <code>docs/multiplayer.md</code>.</p></section><aside class="guide-note p-[22px] [border-left:2px_solid_var(--accent)] mt-[35px] bg-surface text-[13px] text-muted">Each game runs in its own iframe. Solo scores and preferences stay in this browser. Multiplayer parties use Cloudflare Durable Objects, with guest access and no account required.</aside></article></Layout>
